> to set high value for K and the run time is unbearably slow. We tried
> with se=FALSE and different starting values but there were not much
> improvement in run time. There is no way that we can do our modeling
> at this speed (>3 days to run a model). As you said in an earlier
> thread, the main problem is the high value of K.
>
>
> In fact, we are not interested in the abundance estimate but in its
> relationship with the habitat covariates. We are trying to investigate
> the influence of habitat covariate upon the species abundance.  In
> such case, can we divide all the count data by some arbitrary number
> like 10 or 100 (so that we can use a significantly lower number for K)
> and run the model to look at the effect of habitat covariates on
> abundance?
> Thanks

From udf2ff87426a98fc9 at groups.google.com  Thu Mar 15 21:27:10 2012
From: udf2ff87426a98fc9 at groups.google.com (Dan)
Date: Thu, 15 Mar 2012 21:27:10 +0000
Subject: Guidelines for Avoiding Over-parameterized Models
Message-ID: <yYKE11RIS54.ejfcBMmbjQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/yYKE11RIS54/m/ejfcBMmbjQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I have found conflicting information when it comes to limiting the
number of estimable parameters in occupancy models (single species,
single season for example). One suggestion is to keep the maximum
number of model parameters under the number of unique encounter
histories and the other suggests ensuring that you have 10x the number
of sites to parameters. What IS the rule of thumb in this situation?

Cheers,
Dan

